1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,400
Over the past two years, spot market prices for enterprise memory have surged by more than two hundred percent. What began as a routine tech cycle has morphed into a historic silicon bottleneck.

2
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:20,160
To feed modern AI processors, manufacturers must use High Bandwidth Memory. But here is the catch: producing one gigabyte of HBM takes three times as much physical silicon wafer area as standard DDR5.

3
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:32,359
By mid-2024, memory giants SK Hynix and Micron dropped an unprecedented bombshell: their entire production lines were sold out through 2025, with major customer allocations already locked in for 2026.

4
00:00:32,359 --> 00:00:41,359
Even with wafers pressed, chips cannot ship without advanced packaging. TSMC's CoWoS packaging lines are stretched to the limit, with order wait times exceeding fifty-two weeks.

5
00:00:41,359 --> 00:00:52,200
Because memory fabs shifted eighty percent of new investment into AI silicon, standard memory inventories collapsed from sixteen weeks down to three. The AI boom is actively cannibalizing consumer tech.

6
00:00:52,200 --> 00:01:03,000
As the industry races toward next-generation HBM4, the memory supercycle is no longer just a supply hiccup. It is the defining economic tollbooth of the entire artificial intelligence revolution.
